l` as an input to every batch-economics curve; add a G0.5-style reconciliation check that fails if any pricing assumption volume differs from the brief by >10%.\n\n3. (severity HIGH) — Bill of Materials / Cost Stack\n   Finding: The installed-cost benchmarking text is for a 30 kW R290 monobloc, not the requested 8 kW residential unit.\n   Evidence: Section 6 page 39: “Installed ASP for 30 kW R290 monobloc: hardware (~£9-13k) + scaled pipework + electrics + MCS commissioning = £18-27k total”.\n   Root cause in chain: Reference-anchor / cost-reality text from Engine C or G2 is not reconciled against the brief scale; G0.5 appears to check only headline design output, not all downstream reference text.\n   Fix recommendation: Extend G0.5 brief-target-reconciliation to scan all generated cost-reference prose for capacity tokens and compare against `brief.thermal_output_kw`; file likely `src/.../gates/brief-target-reconciliation.ts`. Any “30 kW” benchmark in an 8 kW report should HALT.\n\n4. (severity HIGH) — Section 1 · Brief & Requirements\n   Finding: The physical envelope is impossible and contradicts the actual chassis dimensions.\n   Evidence: Section 1 page 3: “Envelope footprint 1200 × 800 × 144 mm (L × W × H)”; Module 1 page 9: “weatherproof outer shell ... 960x460x860 mm”.\n   Root cause in chain: Structure containment derived-parameter ledger confused EPAL pallet deck height with product envelope height.\n   Fix recommendation: In G0 physics ledger / `class-floors.ts`, separate `shipping_pallet_standard` from `product_envelope_mm`; add dimensional consistency gate: module bounding-box height must be ≤ declared product envelope height, otherwise HALT.\n\n5. (severity HIGH) — Section 6 · Bill of Materials\n   Finding: The raw BoM is catastrophically underpriced and cannot support an 8 kW ASHP at production scale.\n   Evidence: Section 6 page 39: “RAW MATERIALS BoM GRAND TOTAL £1,762.21”; Module 4 page 44 prices “Finned-Tube Evaporator ... £2.96” and “Brazed-Plate Condenser ... £2.96”.\n   Root cause in chain: Engine B price-estimation fallback accepts nonsensical web estimates and applies inappropriate batch multipliers to major electromechanical parts.\n   Fix recommendation: Add component-class floor prices in `src/.../class-floors.ts` / Engine B: 8 kW heat-pump evaporator, condenser, compressor, inverter, fan assembly, pump assembly, sheet-metal enclosure must have floor bands. G2 must HALT if any major spend item is >5× below class floor.\n\n6. (severity HIGH) — Module 4 / BoM\n   Finding: The compressor is duplicated and mis-modelled as both a “BLDC compressor motor” and a separate “Highly Compressor”.\n   Evidence: Module 4 page 18: “A Highly bldc compressor motor (part EHV-28CC-R290...)”; BoM page 44: “Bldc Compressor Motor ... EHV-28CC-R290 ... £17.08” and “Highly Compressor ... EHV-28CC-R290 ... £113.20”.\n   Root cause in chain: Module decomposition treats hermetic compressor internal motor as a separately procurable item, then BoM extraction duplicates it.\n   Fix recommendation: Update heat-pump ontology in `class-floors.ts` / module grammar to model residential hermetic/rotary compressor as one assembly, not motor + compressor. Add BoM de-duplication keyed on same manufacturer+part number within same sub-module.\n\n7. (severity HIGH) — Module 3 · Hydronic Routing\n   Finding: The hydronic flow rate is physically incapable of carrying 8 kW except at an absurd water temperature rise.\n   Evidence: Module 3 page 15: “Wilo circulation pump body ... rated 200 L/h flow rate”; Module 4 page 16: “deliver 8 kW of rated thermal output”.\n   Root cause in chain: G0 physics ledger lacks a hydronic heat-transfer conservation check.\n   Fix recommendation: Add deterministic check in G0: `Q = m_dot * Cp * ΔT`; for 8 kW at normal ASHP ΔT 5–7 K require roughly 1,000–1,400 L/h. HALT if declared pump flow cannot carry rated thermal output at configured ΔT.\n\n8. (severity HIGH) — Module 3 / Module 6\n   Finding: The report disassembles a circulator pump into non-procurable “pump motor”, “pump impeller”, “pump volute”, “mechanical shaft seal”, etc., creating an unrealistic BoM.\n   Evidence: Module 4 page 18: “A Grundfos ec pump motor (part UPM3-60W...)”; Module 6 page 22: “A Grundfos pump impeller ... A Grundfos pump volute housing ... A John Crane mechanical shaft seal”.\n   Root cause in chain: Procurement abstraction layer decomposes COTS assemblies into fantasy subcomponents.\n   Fix recommendation: Add a COTS assembly rule in BoM generation: for catalog pumps/fans/compressors, procure complete assemblies unless the brief explicitly asks for internal manufacturing. Enforce in `part-verification` and Engine B by class `cots_mechanical_assembly`.\n\n9. (severity HIGH) — Appendix A / Bill of Materials\n   Finding: The report says fabricated SKUs were stripped, but the BoM still prices and totals those fabricated SKUs.\n   Evidence: Appendix A page 51: “Stripped (fakes): 67”; Section 6 page 44 still includes “Sanhua EV-8K-R290”, “SWEP B5-24-R290”, “Highly EHV-28CC-R290”, etc. in priced BoM totals.\n   Root cause in chain: Stage 4.5 part verification writes an appendix but does not mutate or invalidate the canonical BoM used for costing.\n   Fix recommendation: In `part-verification` / K10 part verification stage, mark fabricated parts as `invalid_for_costing=true` and remove from priced totals unless a verified replacement is selected. G5 should HALT if any fake part remains in the procurement BoM.\n\n10. (severity HIGH) — Module 2 / Module 6 / acoustic claims\n   Finding: The claimed 35 dBA at 1 m is implausible for a 500 mm axial fan moving 3,200 m³/h plus compressor noise.\n   Evidence: Section 1 page 3: “night-time sound pressure limit of 35 dBA at 1m”; Module 6 page 22: “500 mm diameter ... 3200 m³/h at 150 W”.\n   Root cause in chain: No acoustic physics floor links airflow, fan diameter, fan speed, compressor enclosure, and sound power.\n   Fix recommendation: Add an acoustic sanity gate in G0 or G1b: estimate minimum fan/compressor sound power and require EN 12102-1 sound-power declaration. Flag any 8 kW outdoor ASHP claiming <40 dBA SPL at 1 m unless supported by a sound-power model.\n\n11. (severity HIGH) — Module 2 / performance claims\n   Finding: The report falsely implies the unit maintains 8 kW output and SCOP 3.5 at harsh winter conditions.\n   Evidence: Module 2 page 11: “ensure the system maintains its 8 kW thermal output and SCOP of 3.5 even in harsh winter conditions”; Section 1 page 3: “operating ambient range of -25 °C to +45 °C”.\n   Root cause in chain: Generator conflates seasonal SCOP with point performance and fails to derate capacity/COP at low ambient.\n   Fix recommendation: Add thermodynamic wording constraints in the generator and physics critic: SCOP may only be described as seasonal under EN 14825; rated output must specify condition, e.g. A7/W35, A-7/W35, A2/W35. HALT if “SCOP at -25 °C” or equivalent language appears.\n\n12. (severity HIGH) — Section 1 / Regulatory & Compliance\n   Finding: The report does not define rated test conditions, so the “8 kW” claim is not certifiable under EN 14511/MCS.\n   Evidence: Section 0 page 2: “Design thermal output 8 kW”; compliance page 33 lists “BS EN 14511 ... Rated-conditions performance test” but no A/W condition is stated anywhere.\n   Root cause in chain: Brief parser and G0 physics ledger accept capacity without a required operating point.\n   Fix recommendation: Update `class-floors.ts` for heat pumps to require rated condition fields: air inlet temperature, water flow/return temperature, heating/cooling mode, climate bin for SCOP. If absent, ask clarification or default visibly with warning.\n\n13. (severity HIGH) — Regulatory & Compliance\n   Finding: Mandatory electrical safety / LVD coverage is missing despite a 230 V inverter-driven appliance.\n   Evidence: Compliance table pages 33–34 includes EMC, PED, Machinery, ErP, etc., but not LVD 2014/35/EU or UK Electrical Equipment Safety Regulations; risk page 35 mentions “Governed by: EMC 2014/30/EU, LVD 2014/35/EU” only inside a hazard.\n   Root cause in chain: `class-standards.ts` registry for residential heat pumps is incomplete; G1b only warned on EMC.\n   Fix recommendation: Add LVD 2014/35/EU, Electrical Equipment Safety Regulations 2016, BS EN/IEC 60335-1, and BS EN/IEC 60335-2-40 as mandatory for UK/EU residential heat pumps in `src/.../class-standards.ts`.\n\n14. (severity HIGH) — Regulatory & Compliance\n   Finding: UK MCS compliance is treated generically and duplicates “UK MCS” / “MCS” without the specific MCS product and installation standards needed for a UK residential heat pump.\n   Evidence: Compliance page 34 lists both “UK MCS — Microgeneration Certification Scheme” and “MCS — Microgeneration Certification Scheme (UK)”; status says “Declared in this product brief; awaiting universal-applicability review”.\n   Root cause in chain: Compliance registry stores umbrella scheme names rather than scheme documents and product-specific certification requirements.\n   Fix recommendation: In `class-standards.ts`, replace generic “MCS” with applicable documents, e.g. MCS heat-pump product certification requirements, MIS 3005 installation, MCS 020 sound/planning where relevant, and MCS installation/product certification routes. Deduplicate aliases.\n\n15. (severity HIGH) — Regulatory & Compliance / UK jurisdiction\n   Finding: The report uses EU CE directives as if they directly satisfy a UK market product without UKCA/UK statutory equivalents.\n   Evidence: Section 1 page 3: “designed specifically for the UK market”; compliance page 33 lists “PED 2014/68/EU”, “MD 2006/42/EC”, “EMC 2014/30/EU”, “ErP 2009/125/EC” with jurisdiction “EU”.\n   Root cause in chain: Jurisdiction resolver treats “UK, EU” as interchangeable and only emits a warning.\n   Fix recommendation: Change G1b jurisdiction resolver to map UK products to UK Pressure Equipment Safety Regulations, Supply of Machinery Safety Regulations, EMC Regulations, Electrical Equipment Safety Regulations, UKCA marking, and UK Ecodesign/Energy Labelling requirements. If EU is also targeted, require an explicit dual-market compliance plan.\n\n16. (severity HIGH) — Regulatory & Compliance / R290 safety\n   Finding: The R290 charge-limit statement is not supported by any IEC 60335-2-40 / BS EN 378 calculation and incorrectly groups A2L and A3 limits together.\n   Evidence: Module 3 page 14: “charged with exactly 650 g of R290 ... carefully metered to stay below the A2L/A3 charge limits for residential outdoor units”.\n   Root cause in chain: Compliance generator emits generic refrigerant safety text without deterministic charge-limit worksheet.\n   Fix recommendation: Add a refrigerant-charge gate: refrigerant class, charge mass, installation location, occupied-space connection/leak path, minimum room area/height, ventilation, and IEC 60335-2-40 Annex charge-limit method must be calculated. R290 must be treated as A3, not A2L.\n\n17. (severity HIGH) — Risk & Failure-Mode Analysis / Module 9\n   Finding: High-pressure protection is technically incoherent: the risk text says high-pressure cutout on suction line and the design lists only a 3 bar hydronic relief valve under refrigerant safety.\n   Evidence: Risk page 36: “high-pressure cutout switch on suction line”; Module 9 page 28: “A Caleffi pressure relief valve ... rated 3 bar hydronic relief” inside “Refrigerant safety”.\n   Root cause in chain: Risk library and safety module grammar mix hydronic and refrigerant pressure protection.\n   Fix recommendation: In K10 heat-pump reference graph and risk templates, separate refrigerant high-side protection from hydronic relief. Require HP switch on discharge/high side, LP switch on suction/low side, hydronic PRV in hydronic module, and refrigerant relief strategy only if required by design code.\n\n18. (severity HIGH) — Module 9 · R290 Safety & Pressure Protection\n   Finding: The R290 mitigation architecture is industrial hazardous-area scope creep and not a coherent residential appliance safety concept.\n   Evidence: Module 9 page 28: “ATEX-rated gas leak sensor ... sealed relay enclosure”; BoM page 49: “Gas Leak Sensor MSA Ultima-X5000 ... £13.42” and “Intrinsically Safe Barrier Pepperl+Fuchs KFD2-STC4-Ex1”.\n   Root cause in chain: Generator conflates R290 appliance ignition-source controls with ATEX process-industry zone design; Engine B then prices industrial gas detection absurdly low.\n   Fix recommendation: Add product-class rule: residential outdoor monobloc R290 safety should be based on IEC 60335-2-40 / EN 378 appliance requirements, ignition-source assessment, ventilation/opening geometry, and sealed/enclosed electrical compartments. Do not introduce ATEX/IS barriers unless hazardous-area classification is explicitly required.\n\n19. (severity HIGH) — Risk & Failure-Mode Analysis\n   Finding: The service/decommissioning hazard incorrectly applies F-gas certification and F-gas register logic to R290 propane.\n   Evidence: Risk page 36: “Typical mitigations: F-gas-certified installer requirement ... F-gas register tracking”; Section 1 says refrigerant is “R290 (propane)”.\n   Root cause in chain: Risk template assumes all refrigerants are fluorinated gases.\n   Fix recommendation: Parameterise CHE-02 risk text by refrigerant family. For hydrocarbons, use hydrocarbon refrigerant competence, DSEAR/fire-safety/service procedures, recovery/safe venting requirements as applicable, not F-gas quota/register language.\n\n20. (severity MED) — Regulatory & Compliance\n   Finding: EU F-gas regulation is overstated as a direct compliance driver for R290, which is not a fluorinated greenhouse gas.\n   Evidence: Section 1 page 3: “It utilises R290 (propane) refrigerant to comply with EU 517/2014 F-gas regulations”; compliance page 33: “EU 517/2014 F-gas - drives R290 adoption”.\n   Root cause in chain: Research synthesis uses a market-driver statement as if