; Module 4 page 17: “SWEP brazed-plate condenser (part B5-24-R290...)”; Appendix B page 73: “specific configuration ‘B5-24-R290’ does not appear”.\n   Root cause in chain: Generator invented plausible-looking HVAC part numbers; part verification detected this but did not invalidate the design.\n   Fix recommendation: Change G5 from manual warning to procurement HALT for core thermodynamic parts: compressor, evaporator, condenser, EXV, reversing valve, safety switches. Fabricated core parts must trigger repair/replacement before PDF render.\n\n24. (severity HIGH) — Module 3 / Module 9\n   Finding: The report claims all refrigerant joints are brazed yet includes Schrader/service valves and other mechanical leak points without reconciling R290 leak-risk requirements.\n   Evidence: Module 3 page 14: “All brazed joints utilise Harris Stay-Silv-15...” and “Service access is secured with Parker ... Schrader caps”; Module 9 page 28: “2x Parker service valve ... 3/8 inch SAE flare”.\n   Root cause in chain: Safety critic does not enforce consistency between “sealed brazed R290 circuit” design intent and service-port / flare-fitting choices.\n   Fix recommendation: Add R290 sealed-system rule: minimise detachable joints; any flare/service valve must be justified, located outdoors, leak-tested, and included in EN 378 / IEC 60335-2-40 leak assessment.\n\n25. (severity MED) — Regulatory & Compliance\n   Finding: The report repeatedly says individual bolts, frames, shells, louvres, and fasteners are “certified to MD 2006/42/EC”, which is not how Machinery Directive compliance works.\n   Evidence: Module 1 page 9: “galvanized steel frame ... certified to MD 2006/42/EC”; “TR Fastenings ... bolts ... certified to ISO 4017”; Module 2 page 12: “weather louvre ... certified to MD 2006/42/EC”.\n   Root cause in chain: Component certification mapper applies system-level directives to commodity parts.\n   Fix recommendation: In compliance mapping, distinguish component standards/material declarations from product-level directives. Only final appliance/system should claim Machinery/LVD/EMC/PED conformity; parts should cite material, IP, pressure, flammability, or dimensional standards where applicable.\n\n26. (severity HIGH) — Module 8 / Controls\n   Finding: The control electronics BoM is unrealistically incomplete and underpriced for a heat-pump controller.\n   Evidence: BoM page 47: “Heat Pump Controller PCB ... £1.14”; “MCU Processor ... £3.15”; total “Module 8 — Control Compute Communication £31.50”.\n   Root cause in chain: Generator lists a few ICs but omits relays/contactors/drivers/connectors/current sensing, isolated supplies, enclosure, EMC filtering, software safety architecture, test/calibration, and manufacturing overhead; Engine B treats bare PCB as complete controller.\n   Fix recommendation: Add heat-pump controller minimum BoM template in `class-floors.ts`: MCU, power supply, I/O protection, relay/triac/SSR outputs, EXV driver, compressor comms, sensor front ends, connectors, enclosure, conformal coating, programming/test. Apply minimum module cost floor.\n\n27. (severity HIGH) — Module 5 / Module 9\n   Finding: Electrical safety architecture is incomplete for a 230 V outdoor appliance: no clear MCB/fuse coordination, isolator, protective-earth continuity spec, creepage/clearance, or safety-rated compressor cutout chain.\n   Evidence: Module 5 page 19: “230 V AC via a 32 A terminal block and passes it through a 20 A EMI filter”; Module 9 page 28 lists “RCD protection relay ... main contactor ... fuse holder assembly” but with no ratings/coordination.\n   Root cause in chain: G1b compliance and module grammar check presence of named parts, not electrical protection design completeness.\n   Fix recommendation: Add electrical safety completeness gate: supply rating, overcurrent device, RCD/RCBO assumptions, isolator, PE bonding impedance, fuse/contactor coordination, creepage/clearance and IEC 60335 safety-chain requirements must be declared.\n\n28. (severity HIGH) — Section 4 · Design Decisions / Appendix B\n   Finding: The pipeline knowingly emitted a report with unrepaired structural and cross-module graph failures.\n   Evidence: Section 4 page 38: “Unrepaired Gate on cross module required links ... Human engineer review required”; Appendix B page 76: “K10 reference-graph shadow check ... 1 required edge missing”.\n   Root cause in chain: Structural-gate router leaves K10 in shadow mode and allows investor-facing PDF render after G3/G4 failures.\n   Fix recommendation: Promote K10 required-edge checks to enforcing for heat pumps, at least for safety-critical links. `render-minimal-pdf` should refuse “investor/procurement” output when G3, G4, G5, or K10 required safety edges fail.\n\n29. (severity MED) — Module 3 · Refrigerant Routing\n   Finding: The pipe sizing is underspecified and likely wrong for an 8 kW R290 heat pump.\n   Evidence: Module 3 page 15: “Mueller copper refrigerant pipe ... 3/8 inch OD x 0.8 mm wall”; Module 3 page 13: routes all R290 flow “between the compressor and coils”.\n   Root cause in chain: Fluid-routing generator uses one generic pipe size instead of separate suction, discharge, liquid, and hot-gas line sizing.\n   Fix recommendation: Add refrigerant circuit sizing ledger in G0: compressor displacement/mass flow, suction/discharge/liquid velocities, oil return, pressure drop, and separate line diameters. Reject single-diameter generic refrigerant loops for complete systems.\n\n30. (severity HIGH) — global / Installed ASP\n   Finding: The installed ASP of £3,440.78 is not credible for a UK MCS-installed 8 kW residential ASHP and would mislead investors.\n   Evidence: Cover page: “= Installed ASP £3,440.78” and “£430 per kW thermal installed”; Section 6 page 39 claims this is “Within engineering noise of typical market range”.\n   Root cause in chain: Cost stack applies simplistic installation factor and then uses wrong reference class/scale.\n   Fix recommendation: G